<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teganissorens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1682">1682</a>–<a href="/years/1718">1718</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Decanesora</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teganissorens, also known as Decanesora, was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a> chief, orator and diplomat who played a leading part in relations between the Haudenosaunee, the French and the English from the 1680s to the 1710s. Strongly attached to the French, he led Haudenosaunee deputations to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> in 1682 and 1688; on the second journey the Huron chief Kondiaronk took him prisoner. He became a Christian in 1693, and in 1694 proposed to Governor Frontenac that Catarocouy (Kingston) be restored. Later he received both French and English agents at Onondaga, and in 1703 protested in Montreal against plans to draw his people into attacks on the English, declaring that they would take neither side.</p></div>
